WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.550
A relative bearing ignores north altogether.

2
00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:06.050
It’s measured from your own bow.

3
00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:08.125
Look at the diagram.

4
00:00:08.375 --> 00:00:10.900
Zero zero zero is dead ahead.

5
00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:14.425
Zero nine zero is on your starboard beam.

6
00:00:14.675 --> 00:00:17.075
One eight zero is dead astern.

7
00:00:17.325 --> 00:00:20.300
Two seven zero is on your port beam.

8
00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:23.325
It always runs clockwise from the bow.

9
00:00:23.575 --> 00:00:28.200
To turn a relative bearing into a true bearing, add it to your true heading.

10
00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:33.475
If the total goes over three hundred sixty, subtract three hundred sixty.

11
00:00:33.725 --> 00:00:35.250
Try one.

12
00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:37.900
Your true heading is three hundred.

13
00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:41.050
A buoy bears zero nine zero relative.

14
00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:43.150
What’s its true bearing?

15
00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:47.200
Three hundred plus ninety is three ninety.

16
00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:49.475
Take away three sixty.

17
00:00:49.725 --> 00:00:51.900
Zero three zero true.

18
00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:54.825
Lookouts report this way all the time.

19
00:00:55.075 --> 00:00:59.500
Broad on the starboard bow means about forty-five degrees relative.
